systemd-cryptenroll supports enrolling the TPM2, as well as PKCS#11 or FIDO2
tokens. You can also keep relying on the password you set earlier. Unless
cryptographically secure, using a password that is not hardware-protected is the
weakest approach to file encryption.

It is recommended to have at least two secrets enrolled, one for everyday use
and one for recovery, in case the other gets lost. Neither of these should be a
regular password. systemd-cryptenroll provides the option to enroll "recovery
keys". These technically work the same as user-defined passwords but are
cryptograhpically secure and designed to be stored in a safe place to be used in
case the primary key gets lost.

After you have finished enrolling your hardware tokens, you will be given the
option to wipe your user password from the LUKS header and/or enroll recovery
keys.
